{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2293 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "5a71ffd31d6299faf6852811488229d21f6cdf20e91e952cb8b69f34e21ed13e" :edb/db-before-t 2292 :edb/db-after-t 2293 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535605 50 #inst "2026-10-09T03:25:23.841Z" 13194139535605 true] [17592186124369 1020 #edb/ref 17592186357244 13194139535605 true] [17592186124369 1020 #edb/ref 17592186357265 13194139535605 true] [17592186124369 1020 #edb/ref 17592186357272 13194139535605 true] [17592186124529 1020 #edb/ref 17592186357209 13194139535605 true] [17592186124529 1020 #edb/ref 17592186357216 13194139535605 true] [17592186124529 1020 #edb/ref 17592186357223 13194139535605 true] [17592186124529 1020 #edb/ref 17592186357251 13194139535605 true] [17592186124531 1020 #edb/ref 17592186357230 13194139535605 true] [17592186124531 1020 #edb/ref 17592186357237 13194139535605 true] [17592186124531 1020 #edb/ref 17592186357258 13194139535605 true] [17592186124924 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535605 true] [17592186124924 1100 "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nLet $f(x, y) = \\ln (1 + x + y)$. Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(0, 1)$." 13194139535605 false] [17592186124924 1100 "Let $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x+y).$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(0,1).$" 13194139535605 true] [17592186124924 1145 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(0,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(0,1)+∇f(0,1)\\cdot ⟨x,y-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(0,1)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & =\\frac{1}{1+x+y}\\, &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{x}(0,1)=\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =\\frac{1}{1+x+y} &  & ⇒\\, & {f}_{y}(0,1)=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(0,1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(0,1) & =⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(0,1)+∇f(0,1)\\cdot ⟨x,y-1⟩ \\\\  & ≈\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+⟨\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y-1⟩ \\\\  & ≈\\operatorname{ln}⁡2+\\frac{x}{2}+\\frac{y-1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357209 1017 #edb/ref 17592186357210 13194139535605 true] [17592186357209 1089 "q-129632" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357209 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535605 true] [17592186357209 1100 "Suppose that $f(x,y)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,-1)=-3,\\,{f}_{x}(1,-1)=-6,\\,{f}_{y}(1,-1)=3.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1),$ estimate the value of $f(1.01,-0.99).$" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357209 1113 #uuid "16a5abbe-ff57-5b9a-ab1e-16d413797e0b" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357209 1145 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(1,-1)+∇f(1,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =f(1,-1)+⟨{f}_{x}(1,-1),{f}_{y}(1,-1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1⟩ \\\\  & =-3+⟨-6,3⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(1.01,-0.99)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=1.01$ and $y=-0.99,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.01,-0.99) & ≈-3+⟨-6,3⟩\\cdot ⟨1.01-1,-0.99+1⟩ \\\\  & =-3+⟨-6,3⟩\\cdot ⟨0.01,0.01⟩ \\\\  & =-3-6(0.01)+3(0.01) \\\\  & =-3-0.06+0.03 \\\\  & =-3.03\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357210 1000 #edb/ref 17592186357211 13194139535605 true] [17592186357210 1000 #edb/ref 17592186357212 13194139535605 true] [17592186357210 1000 #edb/ref 17592186357213 13194139535605 true] [17592186357210 1000 #edb/ref 17592186357214 13194139535605 true] [17592186357210 1000 #edb/ref 17592186357215 13194139535605 true] [17592186357210 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535605 true] [17592186357210 1041 "selection" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357210 1104 #edb/ref 17592186357211 13194139535605 true] [17592186357210 1110 #uuid "b33762f2-a50d-51c3-9206-90ba7216864a" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357211 1034 "-3.03" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357211 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535605 true] [17592186357211 1111 #uuid "a3068878-960b-5787-b74d-a95b430e4758" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357212 1034 "-3.09" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357212 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535605 true] [17592186357212 1111 #uuid "ef59c749-e193-5e7c-9c7b-1032889d7ecf" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357213 1034 "-3.06" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357213 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535605 true] [17592186357213 1111 #uuid "5f3f193e-e83c-5782-a3df-93ee78a86b58" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357214 1034 "-2.97" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357214 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535605 true] [17592186357214 1111 #uuid "442f0b3d-ca3b-517b-be61-b040cc120396" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357215 1034 "-2.91" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357215 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535605 true] [17592186357215 1111 #uuid "a58fa64a-02a0-57fd-bb07-25f89f2e313c" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357216 1017 #edb/ref 17592186357217 13194139535605 true] [17592186357216 1089 "q-129636" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357216 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535605 true] [17592186357216 1100 "Let $f(x,y)$ be a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,3)=4,\\,{f}_{x}(x,y)=2x+y,\\,{f}_{y}(x,y)=x.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,3),$ estimate the value of $f(1.04,3.03).$" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357216 1113 #uuid "3e7dfc5c-c144-5489-a8d0-c27c73e72883" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357216 1145 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,3)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(1,3)+∇f(1,3)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-3⟩ \\\\  & ≈f(1,3)+⟨{f}_{x}(1,3),{f}_{y}(1,3)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-3⟩ \\\\  & ≈4+⟨{f}_{x}(1,3),{f}_{y}(1,3)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${f}_{x}(1,3)$ and ${f}_{y}(1,3)$ using the given partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,3) & =2(1)+3=5 \\\\ {f}_{y}(1,3) & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈4+⟨5,1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-3⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate $f(1.04,3.03)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=1.04$ and $y=3.03,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1.04,3.03) & ≈4+⟨5,1⟩\\cdot ⟨1.04-1,3.03-3⟩ \\\\  & =4+⟨5,1⟩\\cdot ⟨0.04,0.03⟩ \\\\  & =4+5(0.04)+1(0.03) \\\\  & =4+0.2+0.03 \\\\  & =4.23\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357217 1000 #edb/ref 17592186357218 13194139535605 true] [17592186357217 1000 #edb/ref 17592186357219 13194139535605 true] [17592186357217 1000 #edb/ref 17592186357220 13194139535605 true] [17592186357217 1000 #edb/ref 17592186357221 13194139535605 true] [17592186357217 1000 #edb/ref 17592186357222 13194139535605 true] [17592186357217 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535605 true] [17592186357217 1041 "selection" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357217 1104 #edb/ref 17592186357218 13194139535605 true] [17592186357217 1110 #uuid "5a02d405-095b-5898-bb69-b4ed45b0373a" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357218 1034 "4.23" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357218 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535605 true] [17592186357218 1111 #uuid "a6ed3bd9-654a-526e-a5e8-911d195ef77a" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357219 1034 "3.92" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357219 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535605 true] [17592186357219 1111 #uuid "f545b7a8-d561-581f-8f9e-4ee3512e215f" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357220 1034 "4.11" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357220 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535605 true] [17592186357220 1111 #uuid "15a46d3c-9ce7-5186-878f-8a5555e987f1" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357221 1034 "4.04" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357221 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535605 true] [17592186357221 1111 #uuid "c27539c1-159a-5b5c-a31f-7db508ef8511" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357222 1034 "3.98" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357222 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535605 true] [17592186357222 1111 #uuid "6f09d95f-6a2f-54a5-8720-087796bc0ec9" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357223 1017 #edb/ref 17592186357224 13194139535605 true] [17592186357223 1089 "q-129637" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357223 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535605 true] [17592186357223 1100 "Let $f(x,y)$ be a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(1,-1)=-2,\\,{f}_{x}(x,y)=2xy-{y}^{2},\\,{f}_{y}(x,y)={x}^{2}-2xy.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1),$ estimate the value of $f(0.95,-1.04).$" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357223 1113 #uuid "722c5cd7-1610-5083-b573-d3d05eef7bb5" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357223 1145 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a,b)+∇f(a,b)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈f(1,-1)+∇f(1,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y-(-1)⟩ \\\\  & ≈f(1,-1)+⟨{f}_{x}(1,-1),{f}_{y}(1,-1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1⟩ \\\\  & ≈-2+⟨{f}_{x}(1,-1),{f}_{y}(1,-1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${f}_{x}(1,-1)$ and ${f}_{y}(1,-1)$ using the given partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,-1) & =2(1)(-1)-(-1)^{2}=-3 \\\\ {f}_{y}(1,-1) & ={1}^{2}-2(1)(-1)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈-2+⟨-3,3⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate $f(0.95,-1.04)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=0.95$ and $y=-1.04,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0.95,-1.04) & ≈-2+⟨-3,3⟩\\cdot ⟨0.95-1,-1.04+1⟩ \\\\  & =-2+⟨-3,3⟩\\cdot ⟨-0.05,-0.04⟩ \\\\  & =-2-3(-0.05)+3(-0.04) \\\\  & =-2+0.15-0.12 \\\\  & =-1.97\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357224 1000 #edb/ref 17592186357225 13194139535605 true] [17592186357224 1000 #edb/ref 17592186357226 13194139535605 true] [17592186357224 1000 #edb/ref 17592186357227 13194139535605 true] [17592186357224 1000 #edb/ref 17592186357228 13194139535605 true] [17592186357224 1000 #edb/ref 17592186357229 13194139535605 true] [17592186357224 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535605 true] [17592186357224 1041 "selection" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357224 1104 #edb/ref 17592186357227 13194139535605 true] [17592186357224 1110 #uuid "85ba5f1c-48bc-517a-9060-647184b38aa9" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357225 1034 "-2.11" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357225 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535605 true] [17592186357225 1111 #uuid "49aecb4a-dad0-5a2b-85d6-19b9a6d5ba6c" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357226 1034 "-1.99" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357226 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535605 true] [17592186357226 1111 #uuid "e62457ba-0612-5aef-a2eb-f78e4a462739" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357227 1034 "-1.97" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357227 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535605 true] [17592186357227 1111 #uuid "becbcc96-86c2-5d37-bec2-864aa6793601" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357228 1034 "-1.94" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357228 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535605 true] [17592186357228 1111 #uuid "52813a06-58d8-5f29-bd42-9bfba352992f" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357229 1034 "-2.08" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357229 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535605 true] [17592186357229 1111 #uuid "19ace5d3-cace-5e78-a701-76bd57fc3430" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357230 1017 #edb/ref 17592186357231 13194139535605 true] [17592186357230 1089 "q-129655" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357230 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535605 true] [17592186357230 1100 "Suppose that $f(x,y,z)$ is a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(-1,1,0)=-1,\\,{f}_{x}(-1,1,0)=1,\\,{f}_{y}(-1,1,0)=-2,\\,{f}_{z}(-1,1,0)=0.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-1,1,0)$ estimate the value of $f(-1,1.01,-0.01)$" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357230 1113 #uuid "fc7ad887-70cb-5594-8df2-c5717f843d45" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357230 1145 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(-1,1,0)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(-1,1,0)+∇f(-1,1,0)\\cdot ⟨x-(-1),y-1,z-0⟩ \\\\  & =f(-1,1,0)+⟨{f}_{x}(-1,1,0),{f}_{y}(-1,1,0),{f}_{z}(-1,1,0)⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,y-1,z⟩ \\\\  & =-1+⟨1,-2,0⟩\\cdot ⟨x+1,y-1,z⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $f(-1,1.01,-0.01)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=-1,$ $y=1.01,$ and $z=-0.01$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1,1.01,-0.01) & ≈-1+⟨1,-2,0⟩\\cdot ⟨-1+1,1.01-1,-0.01⟩ \\\\  & =-1+⟨1,-2,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,0.01,-0.01⟩ \\\\  & =-1+1(0)-2(0.01)+0(-0.01) \\\\  & =-1+0-0.02+0 \\\\  & =-1.02\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357231 1000 #edb/ref 17592186357232 13194139535605 true] [17592186357231 1000 #edb/ref 17592186357233 13194139535605 true] [17592186357231 1000 #edb/ref 17592186357234 13194139535605 true] [17592186357231 1000 #edb/ref 17592186357235 13194139535605 true] [17592186357231 1000 #edb/ref 17592186357236 13194139535605 true] [17592186357231 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535605 true] [17592186357231 1041 "selection" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357231 1104 #edb/ref 17592186357234 13194139535605 true] [17592186357231 1110 #uuid "4ccc4ee4-3761-518d-acc4-76247a58f8b1" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357232 1034 "-0.97" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357232 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535605 true] [17592186357232 1111 #uuid "dc4350fc-cd39-55f9-88f7-6b03cd394f5c" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357233 1034 "-1.01" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357233 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535605 true] [17592186357233 1111 #uuid "074b5d17-1966-5602-a208-7d61a55fe866" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357234 1034 "-1.02" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357234 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535605 true] [17592186357234 1111 #uuid "760f544e-0628-525a-8251-c31e865fbb76" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357235 1034 "-0.98" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357235 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535605 true] [17592186357235 1111 #uuid "39e99e97-023d-5ea3-92f3-933e829deb51" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357236 1034 "-1.04" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357236 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535605 true] [17592186357236 1111 #uuid "01995fbd-842b-5fd2-be5b-65087f7de464" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357237 1017 #edb/ref 17592186357238 13194139535605 true] [17592186357237 1089 "q-129661" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357237 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535605 true] [17592186357237 1100 "Let $f(x,y,z)$ be a differentiable function such that\n\n$$\nf(0,1,-1)=-2,\\,{f}_{x}(x,y,z)=z{e}^{x},\\,{f}_{y}(x,y,z)=-1,\\,{f}_{z}(x,y,z)={e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing a linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(0,1,-1),$ estimate the value of $f(0.01,1.01,-0.99).$" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357237 1113 #uuid "851c10b8-d992-54aa-ac89-df5795dc4190" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357237 1145 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(0,1,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(0,1,-1)+∇f(0,1,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-0,y-1,z-(-1)⟩ \\\\  & ≈-2+⟨{f}_{x}(0,1,-1),{f}_{y}(0,1,-1),{f}_{z}(0,1,-1)⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y-1,z+1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${f}_{x}(0,1,-1)$, ${f}_{y}(0,1,-1)$ and ${f}_{z}(0,1,-1),$ using the given partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,1,-1) & =(-1){e}^{0}=-1 \\\\ {f}_{y}(0,1,-1) & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{z}(0,1,-1) & ={e}^{0}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈-2+⟨-1,-1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y-1,z+1⟩.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can estimate $f(0.01,1.01,-0.99)$ by evaluating the linear approximation at $x=0.01,y=1.01$ and $y=-0.99,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0.01,1.01,-0.99) & ≈-2+⟨-1,-1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨0.01,1.01-1,-0.99+1⟩ \\\\  & ≈-2+⟨-1,-1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨0.01,0.01,0.01⟩ \\\\  & ≈-2-1(0.01)-1(0.01)+1(0.01) \\\\  & ≈-2-0.01-0.01+0.01 \\\\  & ≈-2.01\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357238 1000 #edb/ref 17592186357239 13194139535605 true] [17592186357238 1000 #edb/ref 17592186357240 13194139535605 true] [17592186357238 1000 #edb/ref 17592186357241 13194139535605 true] [17592186357238 1000 #edb/ref 17592186357242 13194139535605 true] [17592186357238 1000 #edb/ref 17592186357243 13194139535605 true] [17592186357238 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535605 true] [17592186357238 1041 "selection" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357238 1104 #edb/ref 17592186357242 13194139535605 true] [17592186357238 1110 #uuid "495c7a33-6298-5ac7-b2b1-ce8c34153360" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357239 1034 "-2.03" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357239 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535605 true] [17592186357239 1111 #uuid "1a1322ae-e430-5629-91b0-4fa9cb4d578c" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357240 1034 "-1.99" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357240 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535605 true] [17592186357240 1111 #uuid "9568e8d8-78e0-5e08-9b74-73c17a96dedc" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357241 1034 "-1.97" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357241 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535605 true] [17592186357241 1111 #uuid "20129a8f-9dd3-5c10-9cc8-a1d3415fa1dc" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357242 1034 "-2.01" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357242 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535605 true] [17592186357242 1111 #uuid "1fa0adb4-1a7d-5b8b-bade-a90b105503fd" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357243 1034 "-2.04" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357243 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535605 true] [17592186357243 1111 #uuid "39eda29a-ea27-5aa1-b0f5-e6c79817307f" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357244 1017 #edb/ref 17592186357245 13194139535605 true] [17592186357244 1089 "q-129748" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357244 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139535605 true] [17592186357244 1100 "Let $f(x,y,z)=xyz.$ Find the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1,-1).$" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357244 1113 #uuid "15c1d2a5-42db-5b28-be3e-79c32c67c3c1" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357244 1145 "The linear approximation of the function $f$ at the point $(a,b,c)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)≈f(a,b,c)+∇f(a,b,c)\\cdot ⟨x-a,y-b,z-c⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, the linear approximation of $f$ at the point $(1,-1,-1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,-1,-1)+∇f(1,-1,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1,z+1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of $f,$ we have that $f(1,-1,-1)=1.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x} & =yz\\,⇒\\,{f}_{x}(1,-1,-1)=1, \\\\ {f}_{y} & =xz\\,⇒\\,{f}_{y}(1,-1,-1)=-1, \\\\ {f}_{z} & =xy\\,⇒\\,{f}_{z}(1,-1,-1)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the gradient of the function at the point $(1,-1,-1)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(1,-1,-1) & =⟨{f}_{x}(1,-1,-1),{f}_{y}(1,-1,-1),{f}_{z}(1,-1,-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the linear approximation of the function $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & ≈f(1,-1,-1)+∇f(1,-1,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1,z+1⟩ \\\\  & ≈1+⟨1,-1,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,y+1,z+1⟩ \\\\  & ≈1+(x-1)-(y+1)-(z+1) \\\\  & ≈-2+x-y-z.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357245 1000 #edb/ref 17592186357246 13194139535605 true] [17592186357245 1000 #edb/ref 17592186357247 13194139535605 true] [17592186357245 1000 #edb/ref 17592186357248 13194139535605 true] [17592186357245 1000 #edb/ref 17592186357249 13194139535605 true] [17592186357245 1000 #edb/ref 17592186357250 13194139535605 true] [17592186357245 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535605 true] [17592186357245 1041 "selection" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357245 1104 #edb/ref 17592186357246 13194139535605 true] [17592186357245 1110 #uuid "622e5437-cc3a-5c20-992b-b46594be4c14" 13194139535605 true] [17592186357